    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Webdock VPS Review 2026: Own Denmark DC + GDPR from €2.15/mo</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">Published 2026-07-21 · Webdock ApS · Verified pricing + datacenter data from webdock.io</p>

    <div class="bg-gray-900 border border-gray-800 rounded-xl p-6 mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
        <span class="text-2xl font-bold">4.1/5</span>
        <span class="text-gray-500">Developer Rating</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Webdock is a Danish VPS provider founded in 2012 that opened its own datacenter (DK-DC1) in Nørre Aaby, Funen in 2024. The combination of an EU-only footprint, Xeon Platinum + Epyc Milan CPUs, NVMe SSD storage, and free DDoS + SSL + backup snapshots on every tier makes Webdock a natural pick for EU-jurisdiction workloads. Pricing starts at €2.15/mo on the Xeon Essential tier; the most-popular Epyc Advanced tier sits at €7.10/mo.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-400 mt-3 text-sm">Starting at €2.15/mo Essential / €7.10/mo Advanced / €19.60/mo Pro</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-1: What Webdock Is and Why It Matters in 2026</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Most VPS providers rent colocation space from a third-party datacenter and resell capacity. Webdock is the other kind: in 2024 they opened their own datacenter (DK-DC1) on the island of Funen in Nørre Aaby, Denmark. The facility is in a "beautiful countryside manor house" on Tværvejen 9A (per their own about page), and they describe it as the foundation for expanding beyond pure VPS into private cloud and adjacent services. Owning the building, power, and cooling changes what Webdock can promise buyers: full physical security control, GDPR-native operations, and freedom from third-party datacenter pricing pressure.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The founder Arni Johannesson started Webdock in 2012 after running Digital Advertising studios in Denmark and feeling underserved by both shared hosting and the hyperscalers. The product philosophy is explicit on the about page: "simply priced, efficient, production-ready, reliable, low-cost, highly automated and with few if any buzzwords and nonsense." That positioning translates to a tight product line (VPS Cloud Hosting + WordPress VPS + Custom VPS) without GPU, managed, or dedicated-CPU tiers.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The combination that matters for 2026 buyers: a 12+ year operating history + a brand-new own datacenter + EU-only jurisdiction + free DDoS on every tier. That profile doesn't exist at most budget providers, and it's the reason Webdock is worth a separate review on this site even though several EU-zone providers are already covered.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-2: Verified Pricing — Xeon Essential €2.15/mo, Epyc Advanced €7.10/mo, Pro €19.60/mo</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Pricing was re-verified directly from <a href="https://webdock.io/pricing" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">webdock.io/pricing</a> on 2026-07-21. Webdock lists prices in EUR (€), not USD — the company is Danish, so the home-currency bias is understandable. All tiers include: free referral program, free SSL, free backup snapshots, free web server control panel, DDoS protection, transactional emails, and email forwarding.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead><tr class="border-b border-gray-800 text-gray-400"><th class="text-left py-3">Tier</th><th class="text-left py-3">CPU</th><th class="text-left py-3">RAM</th><th class="text-left py-3">NVMe</th><th class="text-left py-3">Transfer</th><th class="text-left py-3">Price</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Essential</td><td class="py-3">Xeon Platinum</td><td class="py-3">2 GB</td><td class="py-3">15 GB</td><td class="py-3">1 TB</td><td class="py-3"><strong>€2.15/mo</strong></td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Advanced</td><td class="py-3">Epyc Milan</td><td class="py-3">2 GB</td><td class="py-3">15 GB</td><td class="py-3">1 TB</td><td class="py-3"><strong>€7.10/mo</strong> (most popular)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">Pro</td><td class="py-3">Epyc Milan</td><td class="py-3">10 GB</td><td class="py-3">40 GB</td><td class="py-3">7 TB</td><td class="py-3"><strong>€19.60/mo</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The pricing ladder moves on three axes: CPU family (Xeon → Epyc), RAM (2 → 10 GB), and transfer (1 → 7 TB). Storage is NVMe SSD on every tier; the network port is 1 Gbit/s across the board. Webdock also offers a custom profile builder where buyers can size CPU / RAM / SSD / transfer independently and see the price — useful for buyers whose workloads don't fit the three preset tiers.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      A separate WordPress VPS Hosting line uses the same Epyc infrastructure but pre-installs the LEMP/WordPress stack. Pricing there: Starter €7/mo (2 threads, 4 GB optimized memory, 15 GB NVMe, 2 TB transfer), WP Business €13/mo (4 threads, 6 GB, 30 GB NVMe), WP Pro €24.20/mo (6 threads, 16 GB, 50 GB NVMe). The WordPress line is positioned for "serious WordPress hosting for focused projects" rather than the pure LAMP / general-purpose VPS Cloud Hosting line.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-3: Datacenter Footprint — DK-DC1, Nørre Aaby, Funen, Denmark</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Webdock runs a single datacenter: DK-DC1, located in Nørre Aaby on Funen (Fyn), the central Danish island that sits between Jutland and Zealand. Funen is geographically central to Denmark and well-connected to the European backbone via both terrestrial fiber and the major undersea cables that land on Denmark's west coast.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Most EU-only VPS providers (Scaleway in Paris, OVHcloud in Roubaix / Strasbourg, Netcup in Germany, Cherry Servers in Lithuania) operate their own infrastructure, but a meaningful slice of smaller EU providers are pure resellers of larger hyperscaler capacity. Webdock is the former category — they own the physical facility, the power feeds, and the cooling. That gives them full control over hardware refresh cycles, security audits, and capacity planning.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The single-region footprint is also a real limitation: Webdock has zero presence in the US, APAC, LATAM, or Africa. Buyers needing global anycast routing or transcontinental failover will need a multi-DC provider (<a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> at 8+ global DCs, Vultr at 32+ DCs, DigitalOcean at 15+ regions). For pure EU workloads — GDPR-sensitive data, EU-only compliance, latency-sensitive EU customers — the single Funen location is a feature, not a bug.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-4: Hardware Stack — Xeon Platinum + Epyc Milan on NVMe</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Webdock splits its CPU lineup by tier. The Essential tier runs Intel Xeon Platinum — a server-grade Xeon family with high single-thread performance and proven reliability under sustained load. The Advanced and Pro tiers move to AMD Epyc Milan (or better) — the Zen 3 server family that delivers strong multi-thread scaling and better perf-per-watt than older Xeon generations. Both families are datacenter-grade; this is not consumer hardware dressed up as server gear.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Storage on every tier is NVMe SSD. The 15 GB minimum is tight for file-heavy workloads (a single Ubuntu 22.04 base install + Docker layers + log retention will consume most of 15 GB), so buyers with databases, media files, or container workloads should plan for the Pro tier (40 GB) or use the custom profile builder to size up. Network is 1 Gbit/s on every VPS — uncommon in this price segment, where 100 Mbps shared ports are still the default at budget tier.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The DDoS protection layer is included on every tier, not just on higher plans. For buyers running public-facing services (game servers, VPN endpoints, VoIP, public APIs), this removes the typical €5-15/mo "DDoS protection add-on" line item that competitors charge — and <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> includes the same DDoS inclusion on its annual-special tier at $10.98/yr.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-5: Free Stack — DDoS + SSL + Backups + Emails on Every Tier</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      The "free on every tier" list is longer than most competitors: DDoS protection, SSL certificates, backup snapshots, web server control panel, transactional emails, and email forwarding. Breaking down what each piece means:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>DDoS protection</strong> — included at no extra fee; covers the volumetric and protocol layers that hit most public-facing VPS workloads.</li>
      <li><strong>Free SSL</strong> — Let's Encrypt integration via the control panel; auto-renewal handled by Webdock's web panel.</li>
      <li><strong>Free backup snapshots</strong> — automated snapshots separate from manual user-initiated backups; retention policy not published prominently, but the inclusion alone saves the typical €2-5/mo competitor add-on.</li>
      <li><strong>Free web server control panel</strong> — Webdock's in-house panel (not cPanel / Plesk license fees).</li>
      <li><strong>Transactional emails</strong> — outbound SMTP for application-generated mail (password resets, notifications); useful for self-hosted apps that need reliable delivery without SES / Mailgun.</li>
      <li><strong>Email forwarding</strong> — inbound aliases forward to an external mailbox; not full IMAP hosting but covers the common "I need email@example.com to land in my Gmail" use case.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      When you sum the typical competitor add-ons (DDoS €10/mo + backups €5/mo + panel license €5/mo + email relay €5/mo), the Webdock "all included" model is €20-25/mo cheaper on a like-for-like basis than providers that price these as a la carte.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-6: Side-by-Side — Webdock vs RackNerd vs OVHcloud</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Three providers cover three different buyer profiles:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead><tr class="border-b border-gray-800 text-gray-400"><th class="text-left py-3">Provider</th><th class="text-left py-3">Entry price</th><th class="text-left py-3">Datacenters</th><th class="text-left py-3">DDoS</th><th class="text-left py-3">Jurisdiction</th><th class="text-left py-3">Best for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3"><strong>Webdock</strong></td><td class="py-3">€2.15/mo</td><td class="py-3">1 (DK-DC1)</td><td class="py-3">Free on all tiers</td><td class="py-3">EU only (DK)</td><td class="py-3">EU-jurisdiction, GDPR-native, free DDoS</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3"><a href="/blog/racknerd-vps-review-2026" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a></td><td class="py-3">$10.98/yr (~$0.92/mo)</td><td class="py-3">8+ global (US, EU, APAC)</td><td class="py-3">Free on most tiers</td><td class="py-3">US company</td><td class="py-3">Budget global coverage, transcontinental apps</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-gray-800"><td class="py-3">OVHcloud</td><td class="py-3">€3.50/mo (Starter)</td><td class="py-3">20+ global</td><td class="py-3">Free basic on most tiers</td><td class="py-3">EU + global (FR parent)</td><td class="py-3">Scale, bare-metal, EU presence + global DCs</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Webdock wins on EU-jurisdiction purity and free-DDoS inclusiveness. <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a> wins on price-per-year and global DC footprint. OVHcloud wins on scale (bare-metal, private cloud, dedicated servers) and global multi-DC options within a single provider relationship. For a pure EU-only GDPR-native workload with free DDoS, Webdock is the focused pick; for global anycast, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>; for hybrid scale-up, OVHcloud.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-7: Use Cases — Who Should Pick Webdock in 2026</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Webdock is a strong fit for:
    </p>
    <ul class="text-gray-300 space-y-2 mb-4 ml-6 list-disc">
      <li><strong>EU-only GDPR-native workloads</strong> — data must stay in EU; you want a Danish provider with documented GDPR alignment; backup snapshots, DDoS, and SSL are all included.</li>
      <li><strong>Public-facing services needing DDoS</strong> — game servers, VPN endpoints, public APIs, WebRTC, VoIP — the free DDoS layer on every tier removes a typical add-on cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Self-hosted email + transactional outbound</strong> — the included transactional email + email forwarding stack means you can run mail.example.com with a single Webdock VPS, no SES / Mailgun dependency.</li>
      <li><strong>WordPress sites on managed Epyc</strong> — the WP Business (€13/mo, 4 threads, 6 GB) tier hits the sweet spot for most production WordPress sites; the WP Pro tier handles WooCommerce + heavy plugins.</li>
      <li><strong>Buyers who want an owned datacenter, not a reseller</strong> — Webdock's own DK-DC1 facility is rare at this price point; most EU providers at similar pricing are reselling third-party hyperscaler capacity.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Webdock is <em>not</em> a fit for buyers needing: US / APAC presence, GPU instances, managed services (you admin Linux yourself), dedicated CPU reservations, or large bare-metal capacity (no dedicated server line — Webdock is VPS + Custom VPS only).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4">H2-8: The Final Verdict</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      Webdock is a focused pick for EU-jurisdiction, GDPR-native VPS workloads where free DDoS, free SSL, and free backup snapshots are baseline expectations rather than €20/mo add-ons. The 2024 launch of DK-DC1 in Nørre Aaby gives them infrastructure control that most EU-only budget providers lack, and the 12+ year operating history (since 2012) clears the typical early-stage risk for buyers wary of newer providers. Pricing in EUR at €2.15/mo entry is competitive with other EU providers once the included DDoS + SSL + backups are factored in.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
      For buyers needing global multi-DC coverage or US / APAC presence, <a href="https://my.racknerd.com/aff.php?aff=16299" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd</a>'s annual-special floor at $10.98/yr across 8 global DCs remains the better entry path. For hybrid scale-up including bare-metal and private cloud, OVHcloud's global 20+ DC footprint is the alternative. Webdock's niche is EU-only with owned infrastructure + included DDoS — and within that niche it's a strong pick.
    </p>

